  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nLow clouds continue to blanket central Illinois early this\nmorning...with patchy drizzle persisting along/south of a \nMacomb to Bloomington-Normal line according to 08z radar imagery\nand local obs. While murky conditions are currently in place, \nIR satellite imagery indicates significant clearing upstream \nacross Wisconsin into northern Illinois. Based on satellite \ntrends and the latest HRRR/RRFS data, it appears the clouds will\nbegin to break across the far northern KILX CWA around \nGalesburg and Lacon by 12z/6am...then further south to the I-72 \ncorridor by mid-morning. As drier air flows southward courtesy \nof N/NW winds gusting 15-20mph, skies are expected to become \nmostly sunny across the board by afternoon. Despite the \nincreasing sunshine, below normal temperatures will \nprevail..ranging from the upper 40s far north to the middle 50s \nsouth of I-70. As high pressure builds southward into the \nregion, clear skies and diminishing winds will allow excellent \nradiational cooling conditions to develop tonight. As such, have\nundercut NBM guidance by a few degrees as low temperatures \nbottom out in the upper 20s and lower 30s. The cool weather will\ncontinue into Saturday as afternoon highs only reach the lower \nto middle 50s and overnight lows once again drop into the upper \n20s and lower 30s.\n\nAs high pressure shifts eastward into the Ohio River Valley,\nS/SW return flow will bring a robust warming trend by early next\nweek with highs reaching the lower 60s on Sunday...then the\nmiddle to upper 60s by Monday and Tuesday. After a period of\nzonal flow across the CONUS, short-wave energy rotating around \na deep upper low centered over eastern Canada will carve a \nsignificant trough over the Great Lakes/New England by the end \nof the week. As the flow becomes more northerly, a cold front \nwill drop through central Illinois Wednesday night into\nThursday, followed by a return to cool weather with highs in the\n50s by next Thursday/Friday. While deep-layer moisture will be\nlimited, several models are hinting at the possibility of a few\nlight showers associated with FROPA and the increasingly\ncyclonic flow aloft. The 00z Nov 6 NBM has included 20 PoPs\naccordingly.",
